jobResponsibilities:
Data and analytics are increasingly important to how Government understands the needs of families, formulates policy, allocates resources and delivers services. As the volume and complexity of data grows, FLG needs strong data foundations, trusted and timely information, and the ability to translate data into insights and decisions. AI is also increasingly becoming part of data platform and analytics stack. 

As Deputy Director/Senior Deputy Director (Family Data, Analytics and AI), you will lead FLG's efforts to strengthen its data foundations, leverage AI and turn family data into actionable insights that improve policy, programmes, operations and service delivery. You will oversee the Family Data Hub as FLG's strategic data product, strengthen data governance and analytics capabilities, and enable the responsible adoption of AI and emerging technologies.

Reporting to the Director, OPT, you will work closely with senior management, policy and service divisions across FLG, MSF's data and digital teams, Whole-of-Government partners and technology providers. You will play an instrumental role in shaping the strategic direction, priorities and roadmap for family data, analytics and AI for FLG, while ensuring that initiatives are translated into impact for families.

The role combines strategic leadership, product management, data governance, analytics, stakeholder engagement and transformation. The successful candidate will be expected to connect these disciplines and build the organisational capability needed for FLG to become increasingly data-driven and AI-enabled.

Key Responsibilities
1)	Lead FLG’s data, analytics and AI strategy
•	Develop and lead strategy to strengthen data foundation and use of AI across FLG, aligned with MSF and whole-of-Government priorities;
•	Identify and prioritise opportunities to use data, analytics and AI to enable faster and better-informed decisions, improve service delivery and enhance operational effectiveness;
•	Build partnerships across FLG and relevant stakeholders to drive adoption and realise the value of data.

2)	Own and drive Family Data Hub as the strategic data product for Family Life Group
•	Serve as the business owner and product lead for the Family Data Hub, including the Family Status Repository (FSR) as the single source of truth for marital and family information;
•	Set the product vision, objectives and key results as well as the product roadmap for the Family Data Hub, balancing user needs, data quality, timeliness, data governance, technology considerations and implementation capacity;
•	Drive adoption and use of the Family Data Hub across FLG, ensuring that the product delivers value to users in FLG and whole-of-government;
•	Drive continuous improvement of Family Data Hub to ensure that it remains relevant, trusted and fit for emerging policy and service needs;
•	Work with technical and business stakeholders to translate strategic requirements into viable data products, services and capabilities;

3)	Strengthen data governance and stewardship
•	Lead data governance, stewardship for family-related data within FLG, and serve as the lead data steward;
•	Establish and strengthen processes for data quality, metadata, classification, access, sharing, retention and accountability;
•	Drive adherence to  relevant governance requirements and Whole-of-Government policies and guidelines;
•	Identify and address systemic data quality, accessibility and interoperability issues that constrain effective analytics and service delivery;
•	Build a culture of data ownership and accountability across participating divisions.

4)	Drive analytics and business intelligence
•	Lead the development of analytics and AI-enabled analytics capabilities to provide actionable insights into the needs of families and programme outcomes;
•	Partner divisions to identify critical policy, programme and operational questions and ensure that analytics work is anchored on clear business questions and translated into actionable recommendations and data-led decisions. 
•	Strengthen the use of data to support policy evaluation, programme design, resource allocation, operational planning and service improvement;
•	Establish mechanisms to measure the impact of analytics products and continuously improve them.
•	Explore new approaches, including AI-assisted analytics, to enable FLG access and use data more effectively.

5)	Harness AI and emerging technologies to advance data and analytics
•	Scan and assess developments in AI and emerging data technologies, and identify suitable opportunities for FLG;
•	Identify and prioritize suitable AI and advanced analytics use cases, work with stakeholders to assess potential impact, risk and feasibility. Test opportunities through appropriate pilots or experimentation;
•	Bring together or mobilise the appropriate data, product, technology, vendor and domain expertise to implement suitable AI use cases.

6)	Lead stakeholder engagement and build capability
•	Provide advice to FLG divisions to help them identify and explore how data and analytics can address their strategic and operational priorities;
•	Establish strong working relationships with MSF and Whole-of-Government partners, technical teams and external stakeholders as required to drive data and analytics;
•	Lead and develop a team of data, analytics and product professionals, creating an environment that encourages strong professional standards, experimentation and continuous learning.

Undertake other responsibilities as required to support Division’s and FLG’s strategic priorities.

jobRequirements:
•	Prior experience in policy and planning roles preferred;
•	[For Head-level position] Prior experience in policy supervisory roles preferred; 
•	Intellectual curiosity, and clear logical and systems thinking; 
•	Excellent writing skills to deliver analyses synthesising information from multiple domains, including data-driven outputs; 
•	Experience engaging multiple agencies and stakeholders, with skills to listen empathetically, sense dynamics, and persuade and negotiate across agencies and seniority levels to achieve desired outcomes; 
•	Ability to move between macro and micro perspectives; 
•	Preferably an intuitive grasp of Singapore’s security imperatives; 
•	Interest in current affairs; 
•	Ability to work in a dynamic environment and pick up new skills/ domain knowledge if necessary; and 
•	Proactive, articulate, purpose-driven, persistent.

We may refer you as a potential candidate for vacancies in other agencies we assessed you to be suitable for. By applying for this job, you are aware of, and are also giving NSCS consent for the cross referral. If you do not wish to be cross referred, please inform us via this link: https://go.gov.sg/candidateoptout.

To meet the challenges of this role, you must have / be:

•	Be a quick learner with a growth mindset to navigate a broad spectrum of manpower issues;  
•	Excellent language and writing skills;
•	A strategic thinker with strong analytical capabilities; 
•	Strong interpersonal skills, with ability to build rapport with internal and external stakeholders across seniority;
•	Be comfortable with numbers and basic econ-speak; and
•	Experience, familiarity or interest in workforce development, education, training or public policy will be advantageous.

jobResponsibilities:
The study will see the interns engaged in the following: 

(i) Desktop research and analysis, such as on GIS-related datasets and case studies on relevant urban planning concepts (e.g., 15 minute city typologies);
(ii) Field research, such as site visits to assess the built-environment and streetscape conditions of shortlisted towns and neighbourhoods;
(iii) Planning and execution for inter-agency workshops centred around the research findings, including administrative work like invitations, scheduling, note taking, on-site management of workshop logistics etc.

In this role, you will be responsible for the planning and coordination of HRF, including:
1.	Developing volunteer build-up, reinforcement, and mobilisation plans;
2.	Assessing organisational capacity and identifying areas for augmentation during a healthcare crisis, including sourcing pipelines to maintain a continuous supply of volunteers;
3.	Developing programmes to engage HRF volunteers and sustain their interest and motivation;
4.	Sourcing and managing the HRF budget;
5.	Collaborating with relevant stakeholders on reinforcement training for their assigned personnel; and
6.	Serving as a Volunteer Manager to engage and conduct training of volunteers.

As the Deputy Director, you will lead a dynamic and dedicated team responsible for advancing 
Singapore’s economic engagement with the vibrant markets of Southeast Asia and Oceania. You will 
work on creative and innovative initiatives with colleagues in METI and our economic agencies that 
will develop your and your team’s international economic perspectives, capabilities and experience 
by:  

• Analysing and assessing geostrategic developments and trends that affect the flows of goods, 
services, and investments;  

• Formulating and reviewing strategies, policies and projects that enhance Singapore’s bilateral 
and plurilateral economic relations with Southeast Asia and Oceania, in consultation and 
coordination with other Government ministries and agencies; 

• Leading negotiations and discussions with international counterparts;  

• Networking and developing connections with foreign government and business 
representatives;  

• Fronting communications, consultations and engagements with key stakeholders in the 
business community and general public;  

• Organising and representing METI in visits, meetings, negotiations and conferences.

